.TH POWERGOV 1 "July 2026" "powergov 1.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
powergov \- modular laptop power policy daemon for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powergov
.I command
.RB [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powergov
is a userspace daemon that monitors CPU load and power source (AC or battery),
then applies a coordinated power policy through standard Linux sysfs interfaces:
CPU frequency governors
.RB ( powersave ,
.BR schedutil ,
.BR performance ),
Energy Performance Preference (EPP), turbo boost control, optional frequency caps,
ACPI platform profile, PCI/USB runtime power management, and optional peripheral
power saving (WiFi via
.BR iw (8),
SATA link power, audio codec power_save) when user mode is
.BR custom .
.PP
It runs in the foreground (under
.BR systemd (8)),
as a manual daemon, or via
.BR "powergov on" .
Configuration can be changed at runtime through a Unix domain socket without restart.
.PP
Default user mode is
.BR max-battery ,
which prioritizes battery life on discharge. The user may select
.BR balanced ,
.BR performance ,
or
.B custom
(for fine-grained toggles and on-demand peripherals).
.PP
The daemon uses a three-state machine with temporal hysteresis (three consecutive
2-second samples) and asymmetric load thresholds:
.TP
.B POWERSAVE
Load below 25% (default); uses
.B powersave
governor and aggressive EPP on battery.
.TP
.B BALANCED
.B schedutil
governor; entered from powersave when load exceeds 25%.
.TP
.B PERFORMANCE
.B performance
governor; entered from balanced when load exceeds 75%, unless blocked by
battery-safe mode, user mode, or discharge policy.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B on
Start the daemon in the foreground. Creates pidfile and control socket.
.TP
.B off
Stop a running daemon via SIGTERM (pidfile), with
.BR pkill (1)
fallback.
.TP
.B status
Show logical state, CPU load, power source, battery level, EPP, turbo,
user mode, battery-safe settings, and enabled features.
.TP
.B mode
.I max-battery|balanced|performance|custom
Set user power policy. Applies immediately if the daemon is running;
otherwise saved to
.IR /etc/powergov.conf .
.TP
.B feature
.I name on|off
Enable or disable a subsystem
.RB ( governor ,
.BR epp ,
.BR freq_cap ,
.BR turbo ,
.BR platform ,
.BR runtime_pm ,
.BR peripheral_pm ,
.BR disk_pm ,
.BR pcie_aspm ,
.BR bluetooth_pm ).
.PP
Device layers use a reactive
.B device_aggression
level (0–3) derived from CPU governor state, user mode, battery SOC, and
optional context triggers (closed lid, session idle via logind) — unlike static
AC/BAT profiles in tools such as TLP.
.TP
.B features list
List available feature names.
.TP
.B \-\-battery-safe
.I percent
Block the
.B performance
governor when battery state of charge is at or below
.IR percent
(1\-100).
.I 0
disables battery-safe mode.
.TP
.B dev-log
.RB [ \-\-tail
.IR N "\ "| "\-n "
.IR N "\ "| "\-f"| "\-\-follow ]
Developer command: show structured log from
.IR /var/log/powergov/powergov.log .
With
.BR \-f
or
.BR \-\-follow ,
stream new lines until interrupted (Ctrl+C).
.TP
.B dev-metrics
Developer command: show apply/verify counters and optional RAPL power estimate
from
.IR /run/powergov/metrics .
.TP
.B \-h
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage summary.
.TP
.B \-v
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Persistent settings in
.IR /etc/powergov.conf .
In addition to user mode and features, the file may contain:
.TP
.B PERIPHERAL_WIFI
.BR PERIPHERAL_SATA
.BR PERIPHERAL_AUDIO
Each 0 or 1; used in
.B custom
mode when
.B peripheral_pm
is enabled.
.TP
.B CUSTOM_ALLOW_PERFORMANCE
.BR CUSTOM_RUNTIME_AGGRESSIVE
Fine-grained custom mode behaviour (see
.IR Documentation/configuracion.md ).
.TP
.B LID_AGGRESSIVE
.BR DISPLAY_AGGRESSIVE
.BR CONTEXT_REQUIRE_LOW_LOAD
.BR CONTEXT_LOW_LOAD_PCT
Context-reactive device PM boost on battery (see
.IR Documentation/contexto-reactivo.md ).
.PP
Runtime tuning (thresholds, peripherals, context) is also available via the control socket
(commands
.B SET_TUNING
and
.B QUERY_TUNING )
used by the GTK UI.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/powergov.conf
Persistent configuration (user mode, features, thresholds, peripherals).
.TP
.I /run/powergov/powergov.pid
Pidfile for the running daemon.
.TP
.I /run/powergov/powergov.sock
Unix socket for runtime configuration.
.TP
.I /run/powergov/metrics
Metrics snapshot written by the daemon (developer use).
.TP
.I /var/log/powergov/powergov.log
Structured developer log when DEV_LOG is enabled.
.TP
.I /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/
Kernel CPU frequency scaling interface (requires root to write).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, missing daemon, or configuration error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Start manually:
.RS
.B sudo powergov on
.RE
.TP
Install and enable boot service:
.RS
.B sudo make install-service
.RE
.TP
Maximum battery protection (default):
.RS
.B sudo powergov mode max-battery
.RE
.TP
Custom mode with peripheral PM (configure flags in conf or via UI):
.RS
.B sudo powergov mode custom
.RE
.TP
Disable turbo control only:
.RS
.B sudo powergov feature turbo off
.RE
.TP
Set battery-safe threshold to 30%:
.RS
.B sudo powergov \-\-battery-safe 30
.RE
.SH DESKTOP UI
.B powergov-ui
is a GTK 3 application using the same control socket. Install with
.BR "make install-ui" .
AppImage builds bundle the UI for end users; see
.IR ui/README.md
and
.BR powergov-ui (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powergov (8),
.BR powergov-ui (1),
.BR powergov.conf (5),
.BR systemd (1),
.BR cpupower (1),
.BR iw (8),
Documentation under
.IR Documentation/
in the source tree.
